   145	
   146	### 4. Answers placed with nothing behind them (D520)
   147	
   148	**The problem has two cases, which need not get the same answer:**
   149	
   150	- **Case one:** nothing shown to the judge sizes the amount, yet it names a band.
   151	- **Case two:** the estimate rests on an unsupported step the judge supplied itself (a discount, a growth rate), and that step decides
   152	  the band. (A step the judge supplies is not wrong in itself; the case is the unsupported one.)
   153	
   154	**Options for each case:**
   155	
   156	- Show the band as today.
   157	- Show it with a plain label of what it rests on.
   158	- Show no band.
   159	
   160	No recommendation is recorded among these.
   161	
   162	**What was tried (D565, D566):**
   163	
   164	- **Asking the judge:** asked after the fact which band its own evidence sized, the judge did not separate the answers readers had
   165	  failed from those they had passed.
   166	- **Two reader families:** they mostly agreed on the narrower mark that no amount was the answer's starting point, and split on
   167	  whether a scaled comparable is a basis. That narrower observation came after the fact, and the check's own verdict was "unclear".
   168	
   169	Neither a label nor a "no band" rule has been built or tested.
   170	
   171	**Until you choose:** the band is shown, as today.
   172	
   173	### 5. Two directions the running product invents (D571); two separate choices
   174	
   175	**5a. Yes/No questions:** "Yes" is coloured as good news and "No" as bad. A "Yes" can be bad for the company. Change it, or keep it.
   176	
   177	**5b. Theme ranges:** the highest range is scored +2 by inference. For a theme such as memory prices, higher may be bad. Change it,
   178	or keep it.
   179	
   180	**What the new system does instead:**
   181	
   182	- Its events, and its theme ranges with no registered favourable direction, are shown in neutral colours, with no score.
   183	- An Idea's combined score is not computed when one of these is among its drivers, and the Idea card says which one.
   184	
   185	### 6. Running the new system live
   186	
   187	**Where it stands now (D594 to D603):** the design was resumed under D439, agreed, built and tested on test copies at no cost. Nothing
   188	is running live and nothing has been restarted.
   189	
   190	**What the tests show:**
   191	
   192	- While the new system writes a day, the website and the agent tools only ever show whole finished days.
   193	- On a brand-new database's first day, they say "not ready yet" instead of showing a half-done day (open defect 10 is fixed).
   194	- Only one writer can work on a database at a time.
   195	- A copy for checking is taken safely, and a crash or a full disk loses nothing that was already finished.
   196	
   197	**Example:** the test re-ran six months of one draw while asking the website for the library about 170 times. Every answer was a
   198	whole finished day, never part of one.
   199	
   200	**Three choices are yours:**
   201	
   202	1. **How live data is read.** Today's working rule never opens a running database directly, only a copy (AGENTS §9). The product's
   203	   own readers would need an exception (P1). This is proposed, not decided.
   204	2. **Restarting the running service.** It picks up the new behaviour only on a restart. Example: a date after the last finished day
   205	   would then be refused instead of answered from a half-done day.
   206	3. **When to adopt.** Adopting the new system at all still comes after the quality gates.
   207	
   208	**One small gap remains your choice:** if the page cannot reach the service, it quietly keeps the copy built into the page, as
   209	specified. A "collected snapshot" note is a possible change.
   210	

